  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/6EB41A">Remark <span data-tag="6EB41A">2.4.1.9</span>.</a> Let $p: X \rightarrow S$ be an inner fibration of simplicial sets, $x$ a vertex of $X$, and $\overline{f}: \overline{x}' \rightarrow p(x)$ an edge of $S$ ending at $p(x)$. There may exist many $p$-Cartesian edges $f: x' \rightarrow x$ of $X$ with $p(f) = \overline{f}$. However, there is a sense in which any two such edges having the same target $x$ are equivalent to one another. Namely, any $p$-Cartesian edge $f: x' \rightarrow x$ lifting $\overline{f}$ can be regarded as a final object of the $\infty $-category $X_{/x} \times _{ S_{/p(x)} } \{  \overline{f} \} $, and is therefore determined up to equivalence by $\overline{f}$ and $x$. </p>
